<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mont-Blanc (department)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1792–1815</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mont-Blanc was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> created in November 1792 after French revolutionary troops occupied the Duchy of Savoy, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> as its capital. It took its name from the highest mountain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Parts of it were transferred to the new department of Léman in 1798, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s fall the territory was returned to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1814–1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mont-blanc-department.jpg" alt="Mont-Blanc (department)" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Excommunicato (original version by Naturals) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mont-Blanc_departement_(1812).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
